# BUG-104 regression: an unknown `@directive` must be REPORTED, not silently ignored.
#
# The original defect was silence — AstBuilder dropped an unrecognised directive with
# no output at all, so a typo'd `@reflectabel` behaved exactly like no annotation and
# the program compiled to something the author did not ask for.
#
# The fix is a WARNING rather than an error, deliberately: an unknown directive is
# forward-compatibility surface (a newer directive read by an older compiler), so
# refusing outright would make the language harder to evolve than the defect warrants.
# What matters is that it is not SILENT.
#
# Asserted with `smoke_warn`, which requires the compile to SUCCEED and the message to
# appear — both halves. A plain smoke_run would pass on the broken compiler too, since
# the broken behaviour also compiled and ran.
@bogusdirective
def main()
    print("bug104: OK")
